@chapter 16 | Poverty and Unemployment

@intro
This chapter covers **poverty**: its types, the **poverty line** and the committees that drew it (Alagh, Lakdawala, Tendulkar, Rangarajan), state-wise poverty, the **Multidimensional Poverty Index**, theories of poverty and inequality (vicious circle, culture of poverty, Lorenz curve), and **unemployment**: its kinds, measurement by the NSSO and PLFS, and its causes. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 16.1 | What Poverty Is

@q 11:1-3

@q 11:4-6

@table
Poverty type | Meaning
**Absolute poverty** | Lack of basic needs (food, water, shelter, health, education); defined by the **UN World Summit, Copenhagen, 1995**
**Relative poverty** | Being poor compared with others in society
**Subjective poverty** | People's own sense of being poor
Income poverty vs **human poverty** | UNDP's two categories
World Bank extreme poverty line | **$3.00 a day** (2021 PPP; from June 2025; earlier $2.15)
@end

**Social indicators** of poverty are illiteracy, lack of **safe water**, health care and **jobs**; the size of a house is an economic, not social, indicator. **Gunnar Myrdal** wrote *The Challenge of World Poverty* (1970) and *Asian Drama*. The **International Day for the Eradication of Poverty** is **17 October**.

@section 16.2 | The Poverty Line

@q 11:7-10

@src 11:11
@pyq U.P. U.D.A./L.D.A. (Pre) 2013
The Government of India constituted a committee to set new standards for the estimation of population living below the poverty line. Who was the chairman of this committee?
(a) Nirmala Deshpandey
(b) V. Siddharth
(c) Suresh Tendulkar
(d) Proffessor Jankiraman
@ans (c)
@note The **Suresh Tendulkar** expert group was set up in **2005** and reported in **2009**; it dropped the calorie norm and used a wider consumption basket (food, health, education).
@end

@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2018
Which of the following is measured by the Lorenz curve?
(a) Illiteracy
(b) Unemployment
(c) Population growth rate
(d) Inequality of income
@ans (d)
@note The source prints this as Q12 without a number. The **Lorenz curve** (Max O. Lorenz, 1905) plots the cumulative share of income against the cumulative share of people; the gap from the line of equality gives the **Gini coefficient** (0 = equal, 1 = fully unequal).
@end
@src 11:12

@q 11:13-18

@table
Committee | Year | Basis | Poverty estimate
**Task Force (Y.K. Alagh)** | **1979** | **Calories only**: **2,400 rural, 2,100 urban** | -
**Lakdawala** | **1993** | Calorie-based line, state prices (CPI-AL, CPI-IW) | 36% (1993-94)
**Tendulkar** | **2005-09** | Consumption basket (food, health, education); **MRP** data | **37.2%** (2004-05); **21.9%** (2011-12)
**Rangarajan** | **2012-14** | Food + non-food; Rs. 32 (rural) and Rs. 47 (urban) a day | 29.5% (2011-12)
**S.R. Hashim** | 2010-12 | Identifying **urban BPL** families | -
N.C. Saxena | 2008-09 | Rural BPL census method | -
@end

The earliest study was **Dandekar and Rath** (*Poverty in India*, **1971**), then **Lakdawala (1989-93) → Tendulkar (2005) → Rangarajan (2012)**. Poverty is measured from **household consumption expenditure** (NSSO surveys), using the **head-count ratio**; per capita income is not used. The **Planning Commission** (now **NITI Aayog**) was the nodal agency. The **HCES 2023-24** puts consumption poverty very low (the World Bank estimates extreme poverty at about **5%** in 2022-23).

