@q 11:31-33

The **PDS** began in the 1960s with **price support** for farmers and supply of essential goods at **subsidised prices**; the **food subsidy** is paid by the **Centre**, so statement 3 is wrong. The Targeted PDS came in **1997**.

@section 16.4 | Multidimensional Poverty

@q 11:34-36

@q 11:37-40

@table
MPI fact | Detail
Built by | **OPHI (Oxford) and UNDP**, 2010 (replaced the **Human Poverty Index** of 1997)
Dimensions | **Health, education, living standard**
Indicators | **10** (nutrition, child mortality; years of schooling, attendance; cooking fuel, sanitation, water, electricity, housing, assets)
India's national MPI (NITI Aayog) | 12 indicators; poor share fell from **29.2% (2013-14) to 11.3% (2022-23)**; about **24.8 crore** people escaped poverty
**Uttar Pradesh** | **Largest fall**: about **5.94 crore** people escaped multidimensional poverty
@end

@q 11:41-43

@section 16.5 | Unemployment

@q 11:44-48

@table
Unemployment type | Meaning
**Disguised** | More people on a job than needed; marginal product **zero**; found in **agriculture** in **rural areas** (Ragnar Nurkse, Joan Robinson)
**Seasonal** | No work in the off-season (farming)
**Structural** | Mismatch of skills and jobs; long-term
**Frictional** | Between jobs
**Educated** | Educated people without suitable jobs; fed by **lack of professional education**
@end

@q 11:49-52

@table
Measure (NSSO / PLFS) | Reference period
**Usual Principal Status (UPS)** | Unemployed for the **major part of the year** (365 days); measures **chronic** unemployment
Usual Status (PS+SS) | Adds subsidiary work of 30 days or more
**Current Weekly Status (CWS)** | **Week** before the survey
**Current Daily Status (CDS)** | Each day of the week; best for underemployment
**Periodic Labour Force Survey** | Since **2017-18** (NSO); **monthly** estimates since **2025**
@end

@q 11:53

@qnote 11:53 | The source keys (a). The PLFS **urban quarterly** rate (CWS) was roughly **8% in July-September 2019** and about **7% in July-September 2022**, after a pandemic peak above **20%** (April-June 2020). So the rate was not stable, and the "increased" reading is doubtful. The usual-status rate for 2023-24 was **3.2%**.

@up UP link: UP had the **largest number of poor** (2011-12) but showed the **biggest fall in multidimensional poverty**: about **5.94 crore** people escaped it between 2013-14 and 2022-23 (NITI Aayog, 2024), and its MPI headcount fell from about 43% to about 17%.

@trap
**Absolute poverty defined: Copenhagen 1995.** **Calories: 2,400 rural, 2,100 urban (Alagh, 1979).** **Lakdawala 1993 → Tendulkar 2009 (37.2%) → Rangarajan 2014.** **Poverty line = consumption expenditure.** **Lorenz = income inequality.** **Culture of poverty: Oscar Lewis.** **Vicious circle: Nurkse.** **Urban BPL: Hashim.** **Ethiopia of India = MP.** **MPI: OPHI + UNDP, 2010, 10 indicators, 3 dimensions.** **HPI 1997.** **Disguised unemployment = agriculture, rural.** **UPS = major part of the year.** NSSO has **no "current monthly status"**.
@end

